[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue: the parser reads dates month first. Read
report by report, its <ProblemCount ticket={4471} /> reports show
<ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="problems" /> different problems. Each one has a ticket of its own.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## Where it stands

Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October with two outcomes:

- <code>parser</code>: fixed by <code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code>. Its ticket is
  closed as completed.
- <code>hint</code>: won't fix, because the booking-v3 form is retired. Its ticket is closed as
  not planned.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but not everything in it is fixed. The engineering update does not mention the
<code>one-day</code> row, and neither change moves a date back by one day. Its ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## How the reports were counted

Every count on this page comes from the reports at render time, the way the desk's own cause table
counts them. Nothing here is typed in.

1. **One id, one report.** The ticket export repeats some reports across its pages.
   `uniqueById` counts each report once.
2. **The tenant decides the cause, not the reporter's browser.** `causes.ts` asks each tenant's
   locale and booking form. A report from a day-first tenant that came in through booking-v3's
   form is `hint`. Any other report from a day-first tenant is `parser`.
3. **A one-day move gets its own row.** The desk's notes (`desk-notes.json`) mark the reports
   whose date moved by one day. The cause table keeps them counted where they are filed, under
   `parser`, and so does this page. They also count under `one-day`, so
   <ProblemCount ticket={4471} of="twice" /> reports appear in two rows.

## parser

<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" /> reports from
<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="parser" of="tenants" /> tenants. Day and month swap:
"Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July." The shared parser reads every date month first, and
every tenant here writes the day first. Only dates with a day of 12 or less can flip, which is why
it looks random to the people reporting it.

**Fix:** parse dates in the tenant's locale, not month first. Shipped with #4471.

## hint

<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" /> reports from
<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="hint" of="tenants" /> tenants, all through the booking form,
all at tenants on booking-v3. The symptom is the same swap, so it looks like <code>parser</code>.
The cause is different: booking-v3 prints <code>MM/DD/YYYY</code> under its date field. People
type what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way, day first.

**Fix:** none planned. The booking-v3 form is retired, so no new reports can come from it.

## one-day

<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day" /> reports from
<ProblemCount ticket={4471} problem="one-day" of="tenants" /> tenants. The date moves by one day,
and two people at the same meeting can get different days: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client
in Toronto got Monday." "The invite arrived before I made the booking." No day and month swap
gives a one-day move. This points at time zones: a date-only value converted through UTC or the
reader's zone.

**Fix:** open. Keep booking dates as calendar dates, and convert only times, in the tenant's zone.

## Why one ticket hid them

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports whose subject lines match.
People describe all of these problems with the same words, so the subject cannot tell them apart:

<SubjectSpread ticket={4471} />

Intake's tag does not help either. It guesses from the reporter's browser, and the cause depends on
the tenant. Reports that need a different fix need their own ticket, even when their subjects match.

<RenderStamp {signature} />
